{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json, re, sqlite3\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path(\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run\\\")\nfor draw,calls in [(\\\"old-1\\\",[8810,8816]),(\\\"old-3\\\",[9043,9071]),(\\\"old-2\\\",[8507])]:\n    db=sqlite3.connect(f\\\"file:{p.resolve() / draw / '\\\\''prod/archive.sqlite'\\\\''}?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n    for call in calls:\n        raw, resp=db.execute(\\\"SELECT request_json, response_json FROM model_calls WHERE call_id=?\\\", (call,)).fetchone()\n        user=json.loads(raw)[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"]\n        head, evidence=user.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n[Evidence in view\\\",1)\n        print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",draw,call,head)\n        blocks = re.split(r\\\"\\\\n(?=- e\\\\d+ )\\\",evidence.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n[\\\",1)[0])\n        if call in (8810,8816):\n            terms = r\\\"plus or minus 2%|data.center revenue.{0,35}\\\\\"'$|'\"\\\\\"'$[0-9.]+ billion in revenue|fiscal 2027 revenue|Q[34] FY2027\"\n        elif call==9043:\n            terms = r\"capital expenditures|capital spending|capex\"\n        elif call==8507:\n            terms = r\"(?:commitments|November 2026|investments).{0,40}'\"\\\\\"'$|'\"\\\\\"'$[0-9.]+ billion.{0,40}(?:commitment|investment)\"\n        else:\n            terms = r\"(?:vera|CPU).{0,60}'\"\\\\\"'$\"\n        selected=[b for b in blocks[1:] if re.search(terms,b,re.I|re.S)]\n        for b in selected[:8]:\n            print(b[:530])\n        if resp:\n            j=json.loads(resp)\n            print(\"stored response\",str(j.get(\"choices\",[{}])[0].get(\"message\",{}).get(\"content\",\"\"))[:1050])\n'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json, re, sqlite3\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path(\\\"poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run\\\")\nfor draw,calls in [(\\\"old-1\\\",[8810,8816]),(\\\"old-3\\\",[9043,9071]),(\\\"old-2\\\",[8507])]:\n    db=sqlite3.connect(f\\\"file:{p.resolve() / draw / '\\\\''prod/archive.sqlite'\\\\''}?mode=ro\\\",uri=True)\n    for call in calls:\n        raw, resp=db.execute(\\\"SELECT request_json, response_json FROM model_calls WHERE call_id=?\\\", (call,)).fetchone()\n        user=json.loads(raw)[\\\"messages\\\"][1][\\\"content\\\"]\n        head, evidence=user.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n[Evidence in view\\\",1)\n        print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",draw,call,head)\n        blocks = re.split(r\\\"\\\\n(?=- e\\\\d+ )\\\",evidence.split(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n[\\\",1)[0])\n        if call in (8810,8816):\n            terms = r\\\"plus or minus 2%|data.center revenue.{0,35}\\\\\"'$|'\"\\\\\"'$[0-9.]+ billion in revenue|fiscal 2027 revenue|Q[34] FY2027\"\n        elif call==9043:\n            terms = r\"capital expenditures|capital spending|capex\"\n        elif call==8507:\n            terms = r\"(?:commitments|November 2026|investments).{0,40}'\"\\\\\"'$|'\"\\\\\"'$[0-9.]+ billion.{0,40}(?:commitment|investment)\"\n        else:\n            terms = r\"(?:vera|CPU).{0,60}'\"\\\\\"'$\"\n        selected=[b for b in blocks[1:] if re.search(terms,b,re.I|re.S)]\n        for b in selected[:8]:\n            print(b[:530])\n        if resp:\n            j=json.loads(resp)\n            print(\"stored response\",str(j.get(\"choices\",[{}])[0].get(\"message\",{}).get(\"content\",\"\"))[:1050])\n'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"\n old-1 8810 Thesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA total revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What will NVIDIA's total revenue be in FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $431.2 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Total revenue is at least $392 billion and below $431.2 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Total revenue is at least $352.8 billion and below $392 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Total revenue is at least $313.6 billion and below $352.8 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Total revenue is below $313.6 billion.\n- e111 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 revenue of $81.62 billion, up 85% year over year.\n    Quote: First-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues climbed 85% year over year to $81.62 billion, while non-GAAP earnings per share jumped 140% to $1.87.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e273, e274, e326, e478, e1401, e1425; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2452 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:835fa8a6ac898ca3973d586c 2026-08-23] NVIDIA guided to $65 billion in Q4 FY2026 revenue and reported actual revenue of $68 billion.\n    Quote: For Q4 FY 2026, Nvidia expected $65 billion in revenue; it ended up producing $68 billion.\n- e2652 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027 2026-08-26] NVIDIA guided Q3 FY2027 revenue to $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, with no Data Center compute revenue from China assumed.\n    Quote: Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%. NVIDIA is not assuming any Data Center compute revenue from China in its outlook.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same clai\n- e4333 [third_party_view; fool.com (Keithen Drury); original source Wall Street analysts ; from gnews:7bf2e552fe4c58c4aeaf5b2e 2026-09-06] Wall Street analysts expect NVIDIA total revenue of $411 billion in FY2027.\n    Quote: For FY 2027 (ending in January 2027), Wall Street analysts expect $411 billion in revenue.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4294, e4375; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4594 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst), reporting analysts' projections; original source Analysts ; from gnews:90f3858225bf7e19b8f38f21 2026-09-10] Analysts project NVIDIA revenue of $411 billion in FY2027 and $678 billion in FY2028, a 65% increase year over year.\n    Quote: Analysts currently project $411 billion in revenue for fiscal 2027, followed by a 65% jump to $678 billion in fiscal 2028.\n- e4899 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:13952dff579190ce77353acd 2026-09-14] NVIDIA guided to approximately $108 billion in total revenue, plus or minus 2%, for FY2027 Q3, implying 12% sequential growth from the midpoint.\n    Quote: Looking ahead, NVIDIA expects revenues to reach approximately $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, representing 12% sequential growth from the midpoint.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as\n- e4963 [party_statement; Stephen Guilfoyle; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2fc71ee9730f9420d6d130bd 2026-09-14] NVIDIA projected current-quarter revenue of $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, versus consensus of $104.6 billion; the projection implied roughly 90% year-over-year growth.\n    Quote: For the current quarter, CEO Jensen Huang’s firm projected revenue of $108.0 billion (+/-2%) vs. consensus for $104.6 billion. That would be good for growth of roughly 90% and Nvidia Is known for beating its guidance.\n    Opinion\n- e4990 [third_party_view; Zacks.com analyst; original source Zacks.com analyst 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks consensus estimates put NVIDIA fiscal 2027 revenue growth at 87.4% year over year and EPS growth at 93.5%.\n    Quote: The Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVDA’s current fiscal 2027 revenues and EPS indicates a year-over-year surge of 87.4% and 93.5%, respectively.\nstored response {\n  \"rung\": 2,\n  \"reasons\": \"The reference is Wall Street consensus of $411 billion, which is rung 2. The evidence-based estimate is about $404 billion, also rung 2: it is below the rung 1 boundary of $431.2 billion but above the rung 3 boundary of $392 billion. NVIDIA's 70% full-year growth outlook would imply about $367 billion from FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion, but that would require a markedly lower Q4 than the 65% Q4 growth estimate; the estimate and management's stated Q4 acceleration support the higher quarterly run-rate. Supply constraints and long channel lead times temper the outlook, but no quantified reduction or cancellation changes that calculation.\",\n  \"cites\": [\n    \"e111\",\n    \"e2649\",\n    \"e2652\",\n    \"e2452\",\n    \"e4273\",\n    \"e5054\",\n    \"e5055\",\n    \"e5056\",\n    \"e5072\",\n    \"e5162\",\n    \"e3890\",\n    \"e4283\",\n    \"e5067\",\n    \"e5205\",\n    \"e5207\"\n  ],\n  \"cites_views\": [],\n  \"pushes\": [\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e111\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e2649\",\n      \"push\": \"towa\n\n old-1 8816 Thesis v8 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Data Center segment revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $400 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $350 billion and less than $400 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $300 billion and less than $350 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is at least $250 billion and less than $300 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 Data Center revenue is less than $250 billion.\n- e109 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA’s Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenue in fiscal Q1 2027, equal to 92% of total sales.\n    Quote: During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e327, e1691, e1931; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2295 [third_party_view; Morningstar (analyst); original source Morningstar 2026-08-19; from gnews:7ce47efd62fa17b9aa29e258 2026-08-19] Morningstar cites NVIDIA Data Center revenue of $3 billion in FY2020 and $194 billion in FY2026, and estimates $361 billion in FY2027, up 86% year over year.\n    Quote: Nvidia’s DC business has achieved exponential growth already, rising from $3 billion in fiscal 2020 to $194 billion in fiscal 2026, and we estimate it will be $361 billion in fiscal 2027, representing 86% annual growth.\n- e2650 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $89.0 billion, up 117% year over year.\n    Quote: Data Center revenue of $89.0 billion, up 117% from a year ago\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2668; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2681 [party_statement; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from extra:2799d8731e89b90c9bd3f108 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said its Q3 FY2027 outlook assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China.\n    Quote: NVIDIA is not assuming any Data Center compute revenue from China in its outlook.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1639, e2715, e3045, e4450; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2720 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA guided Q3 FY2027 total revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\n    Quote: Total revenue is expected to be $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3400; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2721 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of Data Center revenue in Q3 FY2027.\n    Quote: We see Vera Rubin accounting for about 20% of data center revenue in Q3.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2882, e3264, e3706, e4343; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e2723 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000073:q2fy27cfocommentary.htm 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 Data Center revenue of $89.0 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially, driven by the Blackwell Ultra ramp.\n    Quote: Data Center revenue for the second quarter was a record $89.0 billion, up 117% from a year ago and up 18% sequentially, driven by the ramp of our Blackwell Ultra infrastructure.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher re\n- e3401 [third_party_view; fool.com (Daniel Sparks); original source Daniel Sparks 2026-08-28; from gnews:387b486b6e29491b9c8506df 2026-08-28] Daniel Sparks calculated that Data Center revenue would be $99.9 billion in fiscal Q3 2027 if NVIDIA total revenue reaches its $108.0 billion guidance midpoint and Data Center remains 92.5% of the mix.\n    Quote: Hold the data center mix at last quarter's 92.5%, and data center revenue works out to $99.9 billion -- about $100 million short of the line.\nstored response {\n  \"rung\": 2,\n  \"reasons\": \"The market reference is Morningstar’s FY2027 Data Center estimate of $361 billion, which is rung 2. I now forecast about $395 billion: the first half was $164.25 billion, Q3 implies about $99.9 billion, and a roughly $131 billion Q4 would take the year to about $395 billion. That Q4 step-up is consistent with management’s expected reacceleration, but is below the roughly $154 billion Q4 embedded in the previous $418 billion forecast; the specific third-party estimates around $361–$368 billion and continuing supply constraints temper the ramp. The implied outcome remains below $400 billion, so it is rung 2 rather than rung 1. The six-month annualized figure below $330 billion is not carried forward as a flat run rate because it ignores guided growth and the Q4 reacceleration.\",\n  \"cites\": [\n    \"e109\",\n    \"e2650\",\n    \"e2720\",\n    \"e3401\",\n    \"e3406\",\n    \"e2295\",\n    \"e4274\",\n    \"e4278\",\n    \"e2691\",\n    \"e449\",\n    \"e4771\",\n    \"e2681\",\n    \"e4905\",\n    \"e2686\",\n    \"e5309\",\n    \"e5310\"\n  ],\n  \"cites_v\n\n old-3 9043 Thesis v6 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the four largest AI hyperscalers\", 2026, due 2027-02-28): How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\nLadder: rung 1 Lowest range: Combined spending is below $500 billion. / rung 2 Lower-middle range: Combined spending is at least $500 billion and below $600 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Combined spending is at least $600 billion and below $700 billion. / rung 4 Upper-middle range: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and below $800 billion. / rung 5 Highest range: Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\n- e922 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:ba97f512051bcb3ff34f5b05 2026-07-22] Microsoft reported Q3 FY2026 capital expenditures of $30.88 billion, up 84% year over year.\n    Quote: Microsoft’s Q3 FY26 capex hit $30.88 billion, up 84%.\n- e1254 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source Alphabet 2026-07-22; from gnews:ac966c48ae5afea2a79acaf3 2026-07-27] Alphabet raised its 2026 capital expenditure guidance to $195 billion–$205 billion, from $180 billion–$190 billion previously.\n    Quote: Alphabet released second-quarter results on July 22 and announced that it is raising its 2026 capex guidance to a range of $195 billion to $205 billion. The company had earlier projected $180 billion to $190 billion in capex for 2026.\n    Opinion, not ver\n- e1549 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, as reported by Joel South; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Meta raised its FY26 capital expenditure guidance to $125 billion–$145 billion.\n    Quote: Meta Platforms ( NASDAQ:META ) raised its FY26 capex guidance to $125 billion to $145 billion\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e414; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1856 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo; original source 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo ; from gnews:61ba718265df905f5dcc9dad 2026-08-11] Combined 2026 capital spending by the four largest hyperscalers was tracking near $745 billion.\n    Quote: Combined 2026 capital spending across the four largest hyperscalers is tracking near $745 billion\n- e2079 [party_statement; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers guided combined 2026 capital spending of roughly $720 billion to $745 billion, about 77% above the prior year.\n    Quote: Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers whose cloud platforms host most of the world's AI workloads have together guided roughly $720 billion (€624bn) to $745 bill\n- e2458 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source The top four hyperscalers ; from gnews:c8ea23246edff9fbbc981992 2026-08-23] The top four hyperscalers plan to spend $760 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    Quote: The top four hyperscalers plan $760 billion in capex in 2026 alone.\n- e2759 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said the five largest hyperscalers’ capital expenditures are expected to total nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    Quote: With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e592, e3353,\n- e3686 [third_party_view; Michael Parekh; original source Meta ; from gnews:8e2fa4cb6d1ac1bfdd389e09 2026-08-30] Meta plans $130 billion of AI capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    Quote: Meta plans $130 billion of AI capex this year.\nstored response {\n  \"rung\": 4,\n  \"reasons\": \"The market reference is roughly $720–$745 billion for the four hyperscalers, which sits in rung 4 (e5014). The evidence still implies about $750 billion: the four-company tracking estimate near $745 billion and the separate top-four estimate of $760 billion bracket that level; company capex revisions and recent cloud-demand indicators support the upper part of the reference range. NVIDIA’s nearly $800 billion figure is for five hyperscalers, not these four, so it does not establish a four-company total at rung 5. The implied amount remains below the $800 billion boundary.\",\n  \"cites\": [\n    \"e5014\",\n    \"e1856\",\n    \"e2458\",\n    \"e1254\",\n    \"e1549\",\n    \"e1649\",\n    \"e922\",\n    \"e4404\",\n    \"e4405\",\n    \"e4406\",\n    \"e2759\",\n    \"e5081\",\n    \"e5376\",\n    \"e122\",\n    \"e1545\",\n    \"e5292\",\n    \"e5293\"\n  ],\n  \"cites_views\": [],\n  \"pushes\": [\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e5014\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e1856\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \n\n old-3 9071 Thesis v41 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Vera CPU revenue\", FY2028, due 2028-02-29): What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $50 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $40 billion and less than $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is less than $20 billion.\n- e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e4857 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Bank of America analysts ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] Bank of America analysts forecast that the CPU market would grow fivefold, from $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030.\n    Quote: Analysts at Bank of America also released a research report in June, saying they believe the CPU market will grow fivefold between this year and 2030, from $35 billion to $170 billion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e\nstored response {\n  \"rung\": 3,\n  \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA management’s FY2028 expectation of more than double its $20 billion FY2027 CPU-revenue expectation, which implies more than $40 billion and sits at rung 2. I discount that forecast because the evidence does not show Vera shipment volume in hand; the new AMD performance comparisons add execution and competitiveness risk, while broad deployment expectations and market-growth estimates support demand but do not quantify NVIDIA’s share. I therefore imply roughly $38–39 billion, below the $40 billion boundary and within rung 3.\",\n  \"cites\": [\n    \"e3660\",\n    \"e4687\",\n    \"e4689\",\n    \"e4692\",\n    \"e4857\",\n    \"e5044\",\n    \"e5399\",\n    \"e5400\",\n    \"e5401\",\n    \"e5402\",\n    \"e5403\",\n    \"e5404\"\n  ],\n  \"cites_views\": [],\n  \"pushes\": [\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e3660\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e4687\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e4689\",\n      \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"evidence_id\": \"e4691\",\n      \"pu\n\n old-2 8507 Thesis v29 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's committed equity investments in ecosystem partners\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much will NVIDIA's equity investment commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: Commitments are below $25 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: Commitments are at least $25 billion and below $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Commitments are at least $50 billion and below $75 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: Commitments are at least $75 billion and below $100 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: Commitments are at least $100 billion.\n- e1732 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya ; from gnews:d024d71d75661d369798d307 2026-08-12] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya said NVIDIA had committed to provide $70 billion in equity investments to ecosystem partners; the period covered was not specified.\n    Quote: Nvidia has committed to directly providing $70 billion in equity investments into ecosystem partners, per new research from Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya.\n- e1970 [rumour; 247wallst.com (Rich Duprey); original source 247wallst.com (Rich Duprey) 2026-08-17; from gnews:127ca150cda794237261a87c 2026-08-17] SB Energy is reportedly targeting an IPO as early as September 2026 to raise at least $5 billion, with NVIDIA's second $1.5 billion investment tranche expected to be made at the IPO.\n    Quote: SB Energy is reportedly targeting an IPO as early as next month, seeking to raise at least $5 billion — and Nvidia’s second $1.5 billion tranche is expected to land there, putting Nvidia\n- e1989 [fact; CNBC; original source SB Energy, SoftBank, and NVIDIA ; from gnews:a95b9986d8697704ffa17843 2026-08-17] For the Ohio project, SB Energy and SoftBank plan power sources supporting 10 GW and at least $4.2 billion of regional grid investment; NVIDIA will invest $1.5 billion in SB Energy.\n    Quote: As part of the Ohio deal, SB Energy and SoftBank will build power sources supporting 10 gigawatts of energy and invest at least $4.2 billion into regional grid infrastructure. Nvidia will invest $1.5 billion in SB Ener\n- e2080 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:a8855077e115780d8a3e8e06 2026-08-18] Bank of America estimated that NVIDIA had committed approximately $300 billion to ecosystem partners, comprising about $70 billion in equity investments and roughly $230 billion in residual-value guarantees or backstop commitments.\n    Quote: Bank of America estimates that NVIDIA has committed approximately $300 billion in capital to its ecosystem partners, comprising about $70 billion in equity investment\n- e2207 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA and Poolside, as reported by Newcomer based on a Poolside investor letter 2026-08-20; from gnews:1e66644cc2e64e6295fd657a 2026-08-21] NVIDIA’s Poolside transaction includes a $6 billion non-exclusive Model Factory license, a $1 billion investment at a $12 billion pre-money valuation, and the transfer of 109 employees to NVIDIA.\n    Quote: The transaction, first reported by Newcomer on August 20 based on a Poolside investor letter and subsequently confirmed by Bloomberg\n- e2404 [fact; Latitude Media; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:74b684fc710dce162d8f4fe1 2026-08-24] NVIDIA announced a strategic investment in Lancium and a $1.5 billion investment in SB Energy in August 2026.\n    Quote: Also this month, the company announced both a strategic investment in Lancium , a Blackstone-backed AI data center developer integrating grid interconnection, behind-the-meter generation, and storage, and a $1.5-billion investment in SB Energy , which builds “power-first” AI infrastructure.\n    Opinion, \n- e2620 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000075:nvda-20260726.htm 2026-08-26] NVIDIA reported $99 billion of equity investments and $25 billion of equity investment commitments as of 2026-07-26.\n    Quote: These include equity investments of $99 billion and equity investment commitments of $25 billion as of July 26, 2026.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2929, e3258, e3477, e3889, e3917; the argument counts one claim,\n- e2687 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Bank of America ; from gnews:0c8197cb92878f4b5dd1fe12 2026-08-26] Bank of America estimates NVIDIA has about $300 billion of committed capital to date, comprising about $70 billion of direct equity investments and $230 billion of guarantees or backstops.\n    Quote: The firm estimates roughly $300 billion of committed capital to date, including about $70 billion of direct equity investments and $230 billion of guarantees or backstops.\n    Opinion, not verifie\nstored response {\n  \"rung\": 3,\n  \"reasons\": \"The reference is Bank of America's roughly $70 billion estimate of NVIDIA's equity investments in ecosystem partners, which sits in rung 3; its period and exact relationship to outstanding commitments are unclear. The stronger company-reported baseline is $25 billion of equity investment commitments as of July 26 plus $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027. Carrying forward the prior estimate's $3.5 billion MediaTek investment and risk-adjusted $5 billion for the reported possible Anthropic investment gives about $51.5 billion. The new report that NVIDIA spent more than $42 billion on equity stakes in the first half of FY2027 is evidence of substantial activity, but it is spending already made, not an additional outstanding commitment. The $30 billion OpenAI investment was announced before the July balance date; Huang also said the earlier up-to-$100 billion plan was never a commitment. The implied amount remains above $50 billion but below $75 billion.\",\n  \"cites\": [\n    \"e1732\",\n    \"e1919\",\n \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
