{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md | sed -n '144,225p'\"","aggregated_output":"   144\t\n   145\t## 5. The blind conformance test (registered here; $0, no provider call)\n   146\t\n   147\t**Inputs**, built by the repaired `paid_gate.item`. Each case's archive, call id, question, period, ladder and unit are registered\n   148\ttogether, and the build refuses a heading whose thesis id or question differs.\n   149\t\n   150\t| Case | Archive / call | Member key | Evidence | Role |\n   151\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n   152\t| P (joint) | old-1 v4 8810 + v8 8816 | NVIDIA revenue company FY2027; NVIDIA revenue Data Center FY2027 | union 296 passages, 79 quotes absent from the gate's plan | fresh evidence; the pair shape |\n   153\t| P-v4, P-v8 (separate) | the same calls, one header each | as above | the same union | the separate shape |\n   154\t| S | old-3 v6 9043 | four largest AI hyperscalers, data-center capex, combined, **CY2026** | 50 passages, 41 fresh | fresh evidence; a scope bridge |\n   155\t| O | old-2 v29 8507 | NVIDIA equity investment commitments company end of FY2027 | 30, none fresh | control: roll_forward with overlap |\n   156\t| E | old-3 v41 9071 | NVIDIA revenue Vera CPU FY2028 | 15, none fresh | control: multiplier and adjustment |\n   157\t\n   158\t**Part A, the checks against registered forms** (code; each asserts its intended check and message, and a schema error cannot mask it).\n   159\t\n   160\t- **A1, correct forms in v13r.** Each must pass:\n   161\t  - $108.0B ± 2% as midpoint and relative, with range_from_relative;\n   162\t  - Q1 + Q2 = FY2027 H1;\n   163\t  - FY − Q1 − Q2 − Q3 = Q4 as a signed time_aggregation;\n   164\t  - a year-over-year rate on the prior-year quarter, with a bridge;\n   165\t  - a whole × mix via share;\n   166\t  - \"over $300 billion\" (from \"well over $300 billion\") and \"65%\" as exact spans;\n   167\t  - $1.05 trillion as 1050 USD bn;\n   168\t  - a range outcome whose rung overlaps it;\n   169\t  - one figure marked for two members;\n   170\t  - a computed external reference (consensus × reported mix);\n   171\t  - an unresolved member with nothing marked;\n   172\t  - a roll_forward with an overlap recorded;\n   173\t  - a valid scope bridge;\n   174\t  - both per-unit directions;\n   175\t  - a same-base family computing from the earlier forecast;\n   176\t  - 12% × 2x = 24%;\n   177\t  - a computed figure with its bound left null.\n   178\t- **A2, planted defects on copies of A1.** Each is refused by its named check:\n   179\t  - 108.5 for $108.0B (C1);\n   180\t  - \"$300 billion\" stripped of its qualifier (C1);\n   181\t  - \"-12%\" taken as 12 (C1);\n   182\t  - \"$300 million or more\" taken as a point (C1);\n   183\t  - \"not be over $300 billion\" as a source (C1);\n   184\t  - value plus range (C7);\n   185\t  - a missing range end (C7);\n   186\t  - Q2 + Q3 labelled H1 (C8);\n   187\t  - a Data Center quarter added into company revenue with no relation (C8);\n   188\t  - money × % through product (C2);\n   189\t  - a rate on another period's base with no bridge (C8);\n   190\t  - a cycle (C7);\n   191\t  - no outcome marker, two outcome markers, and a marker on an unresolved member (C7);\n   192\t  - both a reference marker and a reason (C5);\n   193\t  - a forecast inside a reference (C5);\n   194\t  - an outcome key mismatch (C7);\n   195\t  - a shared-quarter bypass (C4);\n   196\t  - a cross-scenario estimate (C4);\n   197\t  - a bridge converting the wrong inputs (C8);\n   198\t  - an outcome in the wrong unit (C6);\n   199\t  - a source labelled forecast (C7);\n   200\t  - duplicate member answers (C7).\n   201\t- **A3, Luna's observed mistakes** (from both journals). Each is encoded on the v13r shape and is either impossible under the\n   202\t  schema (an enum) or refused with a reason the request states (the sentence is quoted):\n   203\t  - prose outcome, reference, evidence and bridge;\n   204\t  - unit spellings;\n   205\t  - value with range;\n   206\t  - signs on a difference;\n   207\t  - a four-input difference;\n   208\t  - money × % via product;\n   209\t  - \"first half\";\n   210\t  - \"65% growth in Q4\";\n   211\t  - a passage id in inputs and in adjusts;\n   212\t  - a trillion outcome.\n   213\t- **A4, the existing harness.** The $0 gate, the rehearsal and the mutants pass, and the old run reproduces.\n   214\t\n   215\t**Part B, blind writers.**\n   216\t- **Two writers:** Claude subagents and fresh read-only Codex sessions. Each writer works in a fresh context per case (12 sessions).\n   217\t  Each gets only that case's exact outgoing system text, user text and schema, and the instruction to answer as the JSON schema\n   218\t  defines it.\n   219\t- **The first outputs** go through `model.check_schema`, `from_answer` and `check` unedited.\n   220\t- **Two readers** who wrote neither the design nor the answers (a Claude subagent and a Codex session) record two facts for each\n   221\t  refusal:\n   222\t  - whether the exact outgoing request states the condition, quoting the sentence;\n   223\t  - whether the answer is correct against the shown passages.\n   224\t- **A third reader** settles disagreements. Truncation, session failures and checker exceptions are classed apart.\n   225\t- **Reported:** per case, supported forecasts, justified unresolved answers, and refusals by rule.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r51.md | tail -n 28'","aggregated_output":"    32\t| **3.1 · Signed periods** | `FY − Q1 reported + a different Q1 estimate = FY` passes the signed quarter counts. The two Q1 figures can differ, so the result is a revised forecast disguised as a time aggregation. The net counts alone cannot support the promise that overlapping inputs are refused. Keep the valid `FY − Q1 − Q2 − Q3 = Q4` control alongside a case with opposing signs on **different** figures for one quarter. [Proposed count](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:106) |\n    33\t| **3.3 · Product** | `100 units × 12% = 1,200 units` passed today. The proposed money-only guard still permits it. The correct share is 12 units. Keep `12% × 2x = 24%` as the positive control so the fix preserves multiplier arithmetic. [Proposed guard](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:108) · [Product implementation](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:134) |\n    34\t| **3.4 · Sign, trailing qualifier and negation** | For sign, test both `-12%` and `−12%`, and distinguish them from `a fall of 12%`, which the wording currently promises to read as negative. For a trailing qualifier, `\"$5 billion, or less\"` must preserve `≤` through the comma. For negation, `\"not only $20 billion in sales, but also …\"` affirms the amount and a word window rejects it. Conversely, `\"does not in any plausible scenario expect to exceed $5 billion\"` reaches past that window. E’s actual `\"not only still expects $20 billion\"` makes the word-count boundary consequential. [Proposed extraction change](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:109) · [Extractor patterns](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:12) |\n    35\t| **3.5 · Null bound** | Let `A = >10 + 5` and `B = A + <8`, both with null declared bounds. If the repair only allows null in the current C2 pass, B sees a point A during validation and misses the opposing bounds. I confirmed the existing recomputation then derives `mixed` for B, which overlaps any rung. Validate against the same derived bounds used for publication. Test the two-step chain, an explicit wrong bound, and the valid one-step omission. [Current validation order](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:371) · [Recomputation](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:457) |\n    36\t| **3.6 · Source basis** | A source labelled `guidance` can be marked as both the forecast outcome and the reference. I confirmed the equivalent internal answer passes and publishes. The new check only rejects a source labelled `forecast`. Resolve explicitly whether an outside figure can itself be marked as the family forecast, so the marker description and check give the same answer. [Proposed refusal](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:111) · [Outcome validation](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:488) |\n    37\t| **3.7 · Outcome units** | A trillions figure written as `1.05` with the allowed label `USD bn` still passes the unit check and can select a low rung. A separate in-memory probe also passed a same-key one-input sum that copied a `USD` input unchanged into a `USD bn` outcome. The new equality check protects the outcome’s **label**. Register the correct `1050 USD bn` normalization and have the readers check scale against the quote. [Proposed unit check](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:112) · [Sum and rung checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:407) |\n    38\t\n    39\t## 4. Registered cases and what they can decide\n    40\t\n    41\tI verified the archive headings against their stored questions and ladders. The figures below are the dividing points in rung order, in billions of dollars. The counts of quotes absent from the paid plan are correct. §5 should pin the actual ladders and units, since its current build requirement checks only the thesis ID and question. [Registered list](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:145) · [Ladder lookup](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:49)\n    42\t\n    43\t| Case | Verified input | Registration issue |\n    44\t|---|---|---|\n    45\t| **P and the two separate calls** | `old-1/8810 v4`, FY2027 company revenue, descending **431.2, 392, 352.8, 313.6**. `old-1/8816 v8`, FY2027 Data Center revenue, descending **400, 350, 300, 250**. **296** passages and **79** new exact quotes. | New wording in a quote can repeat an old fact. For example, new `e4899` gives the same **$108B ±2%** guidance as old `e2499`. If the test is meant to exercise fresh evidence that changes a calculation, name the passage and the changed input beforehand. |\n    46\t| **S** | `old-3/9043 v6`, four hyperscalers’ data-center capex in **CY2026**, ascending **500, 600, 700, 800**. **50** passages and **41** new exact quotes. | `e2458` supplies four-company capex at $760B. `e4766` supplies five-company data-center capex near $800B. Those are useful fresh inputs for a scope conversion. A supported estimate can also reach the band without a computed bridge. Define which answer forms count, and use A1 to guarantee the bridge itself is exercised. |\n    47\t| **O** | `old-2/8507 v29`, commitments **at publication of the FY2027 annual report**, ascending **25, 50, 75, 100**. **30** passages and **0** new quotes. Both overlap sources are present. | `end of FY2027` is the existing harness key. The archived question names the report’s publication. Record explicitly that the balance is the fiscal-close figure reported at publication if that is the intended quantity. Otherwise the instructions point to two dates. |\n    48\t| **E** | `old-3/9071 v41`, FY2028 Vera CPU revenue, descending **50, 40, 30, 20**. **15** passages and **0** new quotes. `e3660` supplies both the $20B CPU base and the multiplier. | Pin the positive extraction of both expressions and keep the CPU-to-Vera assumption visible. |\n    49\t\n    50\t**A1 is expressible if it uses its own registered fixtures.** The shared-outcome example requires two members with the same `what` and compatible ladders. Neither pair member can share the other’s outcome in P. The same-base example also needs its own family because none of the six blind requests declares that relation. Both per-unit directions fit as intermediate calculations, with the money forecast as the member outcome. The null-bound form fits the schema, subject to the validation issue above. The Vera source control is expressible as JSON but can be refused by the proposed negation rule. [A1 list](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:158) · [Pair declaration](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:63) · [Existing same-base fixture](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/gate.py:261)\n    51\t\n    52\t**A2 and A3 are incomplete.** For A2, the decisive additions are a plain wrong result and an unevaluable operation, a C3 closed-range and open-bound edge pair, the C6 edge pair, C9 cancellation, a value with just one range end, a missing reference choice, a repeated marker on one figure, and the chained null-bound case. The existing `value plus range` plant misses `value plus just low`, which I confirmed passes C7. For A3, both journals add concrete cases absent from the list, including `range_from_relative` with one input, a qualified computed result declared as a point, and `overlap_unknown` without its disputed operand. Retain the exact journal examples and assert the specific refusal or accepted correction. [A2/A3](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:178) · [First-answer arity errors](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/answers.jsonl) · [Bound retry](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/retries.jsonl:6) · [Overlap retry](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/retries.jsonl:44)\n    53\t\n    54\t## 5. What must be settled before the blind test\n    55\t\n    56\t1. **Critical — agree how an accepted answer earns “supported.”** Have both readers grade accepted first answers against the shown passages as well as reviewing refusals. Record whether the calculation passes, whether the forecast is supported, and whether the existing publication check would hold the family. Count a usable joint reply only when both forecasts are supported in that same publishable reply. This closes the actual gap in the pass decision. [Reader and stop rules](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:219) · [Existing publication check](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:165)\n    57\t2. **High — settle the quote and bound counterexamples and the incomplete instructions.** E’s affirmative source, the chained opposing bounds, C3/C6 equality, C5 source attribution, and C9 cancellation each need a fixed correct case and a broken counterpart. The old harness can confirm existing behavior. Round 52 must show that the new outgoing wording and checker agree on those same cases. [Proposed check changes](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md:102) · [Round 50 finish line](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r50.md)\n    58\t\n    59\tUse the remaining round to verify those registered cases through the exact request and checks the harness uses. If a serious issue remains, return it to Alex under the round 50 finish line.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'Q3|Q4|rung|PLAN =|recover\\\\(' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_rehearsal.py | head -n 48\"","aggregated_output":"18:PLAN = json.loads((HERE / 'paid-plan.json').read_text())\n43:    \"\"\"A schema-valid v13 answer that passes every check: one estimate outcome per member, inside its second rung (a part below its\n44:    whole), the reference unavailable. Flavours: 'bad-rung'; 'q3-agree' / 'q3-disagree' (each outcome rests on a company Q3 forecast;\n62:                      basis_text='rehearsal Q3', rests_on=[])\n74:                d.update(id=f'dcq4_{copy}', what=dict(m['what'], period='FY2027 Q4'), basis='forecast', scenario='base', kind='estimate',\n75:                         value=50.0, unit='USD bn', basis_text='rehearsal DC Q4', rests_on=[])\n82:                          basis_text='rehearsal Q3', rests_on=[])\n89:                              unit='USD bn', basis_text='rehearsal Q3', rests_on=[])\n94:                       cites=[], pushes=[], theme_pushes=[], outcome=None, rung=None, reference=None)\n102:        ans.update(view_id=view_id, shape='forecast', outcome=fig['id'], rung=99 if flavour == 'bad-rung' else 2,\n146:            flavour = 'bad-rung'\n148:            flavour = 'bad-rung'\n163:                flavour = 'bad-rung'\n168:                flavour = 'bad-rung'\n181:            data['answers'][0]['rung'] = 2\n364:    check('separate-agree: equal shared Q3 forecasts are unified, and the pair reconciles', rec['decision'] == 'publish', str(rec.get('errors'))[:120])\n367:    check('separate-disagree: different shared Q3 forecasts are refused (C4)', rec['decision'] == 'refused' and any(c == 'C4' for c, _ in rec['errors']))\n419:    check('separate-agree-float: Q3 forecasts equal within floating-point error are unified, and the pair reconciles',\n427:          '110.00000000000001' in section and section.count('rehearsal Q3') == 2, pid)\n437:          '\"rung\": 99' in section and '\"rung\": 2' not in section and rkey[pid]['decision'] == 'refused' and len(rfrozen['attempts']) == 1\n438:          and '\"rung\": 2' in attempts_text and 'T.F1' in attempts_text and '## P' not in attempts_text and 'D01' in attempts_text, pid)\n561:        rs = pg.recover(scratch / 'torn')\n606:        rs = pg.recover(scratch / 'torn-retry')\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'where |basis|figures|operator|relative' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | head -n 40\"","aggregated_output":"19:## 1. What is dropped, and where it goes\n23:| bounds and their propagation (C2 bound direction, C3/C6 open ends) | a source's qualifier is checked against the quote and shown to readers; computed figures are points or ranges |\n27:| `roll_forward`, the per-unit identity, `range_from_relative`, `signs` | `sum`, `difference`, `product`, `ratio`, `share`, `growth`, `apply_growth` |\n36:  a use. Code records it for readers, and an estimate is the value the figures above it use.\n46:| S4 | two forecasts of one quarter | Every number the outcomes use for one quantity and period is the same figure. Compute each quantity and period once, as a point or a range, and use that figure wherever it is needed. (Guidance or consensus beneath that figure is fine; a second figure of the same quantity and period reached on another path is not.) | the family header; `figures` |\n47:| S5 | an own estimate passed off as the market's | The figure marked in `reference_for` and every figure beneath it (inputs and rests_on) is a source, or computed with no basis forecast. None is an estimate. At least one is a quoted consensus, guidance or third_party figure, which may be the marked figure itself. A forecast member has exactly one marked reference figure or a `reference_unavailable` reason. | `reference_for`; `reference_unavailable` |\n48:| S6 | structure: the answer's number | A forecast member has exactly one figure marked in `outcome_for`. It is a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast (the analyst's own forecast, even when it agrees with consensus). Its `what` is the member's outcome key, its unit is USD bn, and its rung is 1 to the ladder's length. The rung's band (lower edge included, upper excluded) holds the recomputed outcome, or overlaps it for a range. | `outcome_for`; `rung` |\n49:| S7 | structure: the calculation | Ids are unique and never equal a passage id. `inputs` name figures; `rests_on` names figures or passages. No figure uses itself. A figure has value, or low and high (low ≤ high), never both. A source has `evidence_id` and `quote_number`; a computed figure has `inputs` and `operation`; an estimate has `basis_text`. Basis forecast is never on a source. There is one answer per member. An unresolved answer has `why`, rung null, `reference_unavailable` null and no marked figure. | the field descriptions |\n67:| S4 | two forecasts of one quarter | For each shared quantity the request lists, at most one figure has basis forecast. When a member's outcome uses that quantity, exactly one does, and every figure of that quantity the outcome uses is reached only through it (the forecast itself may be computed from guidance or consensus). | the family header; `basis` |\n68:| S5 | an own estimate passed off as the market's | A figure marked in `reference_for` has, at any depth beneath it, no estimate and no forecast, and at least one source of basis consensus, guidance or third_party. A forecast member has exactly one marked reference figure or a `reference_unavailable` reason. | `reference_for`; `reference_unavailable` |\n70:| S7 | structure: the calculation | Ids are unique. `inputs` name figures and `rests_on` names figures or passages. No figure depends on itself. A figure has value, or low and high (low ≤ high), never both. A source has `evidence_id` and `quote_number`; a computed figure has `inputs` and `operation`; an estimate has `basis_text`. Basis forecast is never on a source. There is one answer per member. An unresolved answer has `why`, no rung, no marked figure and no reference. | the field descriptions |\n84:| `figures` (array) | \"The family's calculation. Compute each quantity and period once, as a point or a range, and use that figure wherever it is needed. Figures name each other by id; no figure may use itself.\" |\n89:| `outcome_for` | \"The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\" |\n100:**figures[]**\n107:| `what.measure` | string | \"The quantity, such as revenue or capital expenditure; never its basis.\" |\n109:| `kind` | enum source, computed, estimate | \"source: a number written in a shown passage's quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.\" |\n110:| `basis` | enum reported, guidance, consensus, third_party, forecast | \"reported: an actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\" |\n117:| `inputs` | array of strings or null | \"Computed only: ids of figures, never passages, in the operation's order.\" |\n119:| `basis_text` | string or null | \"Estimate only: the assumption, in words.\" |\n120:| `rests_on` | array of strings or null | \"Estimate only: the ids of the figures and passages it rests on.\" |\n122:| `reference_for` | array of member ids (enum) | \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference: a consensus, guidance or outside figure from a shown passage, or one computed only from such figures and reported ones. No estimate or forecast anywhere beneath it.\" |\n158:- The reader brief is unchanged. Its `deciding_gap` question is where readers judge the recorded conversions and estimates.\n203:  - **S7:** value with low; a cycle; a dangling input; a passage id in inputs; a source with basis forecast; duplicate answers.\n225:  - two Data Center Q4 figures on two paths (S4);\n228:  - a computed reference with basis forecast (S5);\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'from fractions import Fraction as F\nwhole = (350, 400)\npart = tuple(x * F(11, 10) for x in whole)\nprint(\\\"S3\\\", whole, tuple(map(int, part)), \\\"written refusal:\\\", min(part) > max(whole), \\\"part > whole at both ends:\\\", [p > w for p,w in zip(part, whole)])\nfor whole in (F(108), F(132)):\n    part = whole * F(925,1000)\n    print(\\\"S8 whole, share, remainder, reconstructed:\\\", *(float(x) for x in (whole,part,whole-part,part+(whole-part))))\nsource1, source2 = F(745),F(760)\nprint(\\\"S4 outside blend via one rate adjustment:\\\", float(source1*(1+(source2/source1-1)/2)))\nlo, hi = F(100),F(120)\nprint(\\\"S2 same node:\\\", \\\"independent ends\\\", (lo/hi,hi/lo), \\\"same input\\\", (lo/lo,hi/hi))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'from fractions import Fraction as F\nwhole = (350, 400)\npart = tuple(x * F(11, 10) for x in whole)\nprint(\\\"S3\\\", whole, tuple(map(int, part)), \\\"written refusal:\\\", min(part) > max(whole), \\\"part > whole at both ends:\\\", [p > w for p,w in zip(part, whole)])\nfor whole in (F(108), F(132)):\n    part = whole * F(925,1000)\n    print(\\\"S8 whole, share, remainder, reconstructed:\\\", *(float(x) for x in (whole,part,whole-part,part+(whole-part))))\nsource1, source2 = F(745),F(760)\nprint(\\\"S4 outside blend via one rate adjustment:\\\", float(source1*(1+(source2/source1-1)/2)))\nlo, hi = F(100),F(120)\nprint(\\\"S2 same node:\\\", \\\"independent ends\\\", (lo/hi,hi/lo), \\\"same input\\\", (lo/lo,hi/hi))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"S3 (350, 400) (385, 440) written refusal: False part > whole at both ends: [True, True]\nS8 whole, share, remainder, reconstructed: 108.0 99.9 8.1 108.0\nS8 whole, share, remainder, reconstructed: 132.0 122.1 9.9 132.0\nS4 outside blend via one rate adjustment: 752.5\nS2 same node: independent ends (Fraction(5, 6), Fraction(6, 5)) same input (Fraction(1, 1), Fraction(1, 1))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_39","type":"agent_message","text":"**Not ready.** The revised rules can reject a correct sum of two Data Center quarters and accept a Data Center forecast that is larger than company revenue at every possible value. Both are within D737’s scope. A4 should be the decisive $0 check once the written rules and their test cases agree.\n\n## Blocking findings, ranked by severity\n\n1. **High · (a), (c), (d) · S8 confuses what a number is with what was used to calculate it.** In P, the shown figures support calculating Q3 Data Center revenue as `share($108B, 92.5%) = $99.9B`. If Q4 is calculated the same way, adding the two Data Center quarters is valid. Yet each operand **is** Data Center revenue and **uses** company revenue, so the written rule rejects the sum. It also rejects adding a segment back to the remainder calculated as `company − segment`, even though that reconstructs the company total. Conversely, the separate P-v4 request loses the relation entirely because `only()` clears it. A company answer can then add Data Center revenue into total revenue without S8 firing on that first reply. The rule needs to distinguish an operand that adds an already-counted part from one that merely uses the whole to calculate a share or remainder. [SMALL-DESIGN.md:45](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:45) · [paid_gate.py:63](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:63)\n\n2. **High · (a), (c), (d) · S4 can reject two external inputs and miss two explicit own forecasts.** S shows a near-$745B tracking estimate (`e1856`) and a $760B outside estimate (`e2458`) for the same four companies’ CY2026 capex. Using both to calculate a single $752.5B starting point before estimating the data-center share is a valid, scenario-free calculation. The two outside figures have the same key, and S4’s exception names guidance and consensus only. In the other direction, two explicitly marked Q3 forecasts can sit in an annual estimate’s `rests_on` lists and be invisible to S4. The old Q3 agreement/disagreement rehearsal uses exactly those links. There is a third mismatch after reconciliation: two correct P replies can independently use the same reported Q2 company figure, but `merge` only unifies forecasts for listed shared keys, so the merged graph has two IDs for the reported number. Define which figure is the forecast that the answer adopts, which figures merely support it, and how that distinction survives reconciliation. The separate reference in E and the opening balance and later flows in O need no special rule. [SMALL-DESIGN.md:34](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:34) · [SMALL-DESIGN.md:46](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:46) · [paid_gate.py:330](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:330) · [paid_rehearsal.py:55](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_rehearsal.py:55)\n\n3. **High · (a) · S3 misses a provably oversized part when it is calculated from the same whole.** Give P a company range of $350–400B and compute Data Center as `share(company, 110%)`. The recomputed segment range is $385–440B. Both ranges overlap valid bands. S3 accepts because $385B is below $400B, even though the segment is 10% larger at every value of the **same** company figure. Lowest versus highest is right for independent ranges. The shared input makes this case decidable from the calculation alone. [SMALL-DESIGN.md:37](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:37) · [SMALL-DESIGN.md:44](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:44) · [SMALL-DESIGN.md:168](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:168)\n\n4. **High · (b), (d) · S7 leaves cycles through estimate links unchecked.** Since “uses” follows only `inputs`, two estimates can point to each other through `rests_on` without either figure using itself. The legacy structural check follows those links. The new rule needs to say that cycle detection follows both link types, while arithmetic follows `inputs`. [SMALL-DESIGN.md:34](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:34) · [SMALL-DESIGN.md:49](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:49) · [calc_check.py:310](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:310) · [calc_check.py:343](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:343)\n\n5. **High · (e) · The blind test still has conflicting pass conditions and reader inputs.** A1 still requires `$4.5 billion ± 3%` as two sources, while S1 requires the complete expression as one source. A2 says each entry is refused, then adds an estimate that must be accepted and recorded. The final A3 stop line still allows a mistake that is merely “stated.” And §5 tells readers to mark broken structure without clearly stopping on an accepted reply with broken structure. The existing card builder gives each reader only named passages and splits the joint calculation into per-member cards. That view can hide the two paths needed to spot a duplicate forecast. Part B needs the whole first reply and all shown passages, including for refused replies, before the checker’s decision is revealed. [SMALL-DESIGN.md:175](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:175) · [SMALL-DESIGN.md:215](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:215) · [SMALL-DESIGN.md:244](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:244) · [SMALL-DESIGN.md:267](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:267) · [paid_gate.py:457](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:457)\n\n6. **Medium · (a), (c), (d) · The arithmetic and source instructions still leave different implementations possible.** For `ratio(F1,F1)` with a nonzero range `[100,120]`, the result is exactly `1x` because both operands are the same figure. Treating the two occurrences as independent gives `[5/6,6/5]`. “Compute both extremes” leaves that distinction unstated. The `operation` description also omits §2’s zero-containing denominator refusal and leaves “when its inputs are periods of one quantity” without an explicit sum/difference limit. Read literally across operations, it can reject a correct year-over-year growth calculation. For sources, S includes an actual `$195 billion to $205 billion` range (`e1254`), and P shows `(+/-2%)` (`e4963`). The grammar needs to say which spellings count as the complete expression so a writer and checker agree where it starts and ends. [SMALL-DESIGN.md:42](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:42) · [SMALL-DESIGN.md:86](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:86) · [SMALL-DESIGN.md:88](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:88) · [calc_check.py:125](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_check.py:125)\n\n## Round 52, item by item\n\n| Round 52 finding | Revised wording and verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **S8 indirect addition** | “A sum never adds a figure that uses (or is) the part's quantity to one that uses (or is) the whole's.” This catches the `× 1x` example when the relation is present. The false refusals and separate-arm gap remain. “An estimate that rests on both the part and the whole is recorded for readers” correctly leaves the estimate’s judgement to readers. [Design:45](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:45) |\n| **S4 duplicate forecasts** | “Every number the outcomes use for one quantity and period is the same figure.” This covers two Data Center Q4 figures on arithmetic paths. It leaves the estimate-link bypass open and introduces the refusals above. The `figures` description omits the exception for external inputs, and the shared-quantities header does not state the broader scope. [Design:46](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:46) · [Design:84](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:84) |\n| **S5 reference root** | **Closed.** “The figure marked in `reference_for` and every figure beneath it (inputs and rests_on) is a source, or computed with no basis forecast.” The quoted outside figure “may be the marked figure itself.” That rejects an own estimate at the root and accepts a direct outside quote. [Design:47](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:47) |\n| **S3/S6 declared values and band edge** | The original examples are closed. “The published value of a computed figure is the recomputed one, a point or a range.” The tolerance applies only to declared versus recomputed values. Independent ranges can overlap, and exactly the upper band edge is excluded. The new shared-input S3 case remains open. Also, §2 says S4 compares values while its actual rule compares figure identity. Value comparison belongs to reconciliation. [Design:37](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:37) · [Design:48](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:48) · [Design:154](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:154) |\n| **S2 units and range arithmetic** | “Division by a range that contains zero is refused.” The unit table closes mixed-unit addition, and A2 pins the signed product example. The repeated-input calculation and the missing wording beside `operation` remain open. The field should also carry the result units for `share` and `apply_growth`, and the same-unit requirement for `growth`. [Design:43](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:43) · [Design:51](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:51) · [Design:88](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:88) |\n| **S1 compound quotes** | “`$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%` is copied whole, and gives its range as low and high or its midpoint as value.” This closes the original one-sided reading for that spelling. `US$`, `bn`, `twice`, `near` and non-US currency now have stated treatment. The old two-source fixture and the actual archive spellings above remain open. [Design:42](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:42) · [Design:86](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:86) |\n| **S8 remainder and period spelling** | “A difference may subtract the part from the whole only to give the rest, another scope.” This allows the direct remainder. “‘first half’ is refused by S8 when summed” settles the observed alias. The text still needs to state the same-period requirement for that remainder, and which canonical half-year labels pass so P can correctly express `FY − H1 = H2`. [Design:45](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:45) · [Design:238](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:238) |\n| **Outcome, unresolved answers and IDs** | **Closed for the original cases.** “It is a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast” excludes a source as the outcome, while allowing an own forecast to agree with consensus. “Unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it” removes the contradiction with the required answer fields. “Ids are unique and never equal a passage id” resolves the collision. The separate cycle issue remains. [Design:48](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:48) · [Design:89](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:89) |\n| **Header, arity and range explanation** | “The family header is rendered from the family's data” closes the hard-coded IDs. “Sum and product take two or more inputs” closes the arity omission. The range explanation remains a writer instruction. The effective header still needs to carry S3’s recomputed comparison and agree with the `figures` description on S4. The older header under §3 should be explicitly marked as superseded. [Design:88](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:88) · [Design:93](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:93) · [Design:134](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:134) |\n| **Format selection through reconciliation and cards** | **Closed as an A4 requirement, unverified.** “One v13s reply goes through the first attempt, the separate-arm reconciliation, the card render and recovery.” The existing reconciliation and card paths still invoke the legacy decoder and checker. Include the diagnostic card path, which reads the same wire format. [Design:240](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:240) · [paid_gate.py:266](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:266) · [paid_gate.py:501](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:501) · [paid_gate.py:581](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:581) |\n| **Byte-for-byte old replay** | **Closed in the design.** “The old run's `results.json` reproduces from its journals with the ledger snapshot saved inside it.” The compatibility check must supply that snapshot because `recover()` ordinarily reads the live ledger. [Design:242](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:242) · [paid_gate.py:365](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:365) |\n| **A1–A3 controls and blind usability** | **Partial.** “Each mistake must be refused by the schema or **actually refused** by its stated rule” fixes the A3 procedure. “An accepted reply with a named defect … is a stop” fixes the bad-reference loophole, and the 12 replies and 24 readings are explicit. The conflicting fixture and stop lines above remain. A2 also still lacks the requested missing-range-end control. [Design:215](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:215) · [Design:238](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:238) · [Design:252](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:252) |\n| **O’s intended date** | **Closed in the design.** “The balance at the end of FY2027, as the FY2027 annual report publishes it” resolves the archived question’s publication wording. S’s archived heading also confirms calendar 2026. [Design:98](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:98) · [Design:164](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:164) |\n\nResolve those contracts before implementation, then run A4 with each counterexample paired with its correct counterpart through the actual request, checker, reconciliation where relevant, and reader output. I checked the stored case inputs and the arithmetic examples read-only. A4 was not run."}}
